---
/**
 * @file ImaginesBand.astro
 * @description Movement II's visual memory: one plate of five frames, one per evening of the
 *  cycle, standing between the film and the register. The movement runs silence → voice →
 *  memory, and images are the register memory otherwise lacks — the plate announces the five
 *  evenings in photographs, the register unfolds them in words. That is also why the numerals
 *  here and the register's are ONE sequence (`ROMAN`, data/landing/paths): five frames answering
 *  five entries is what makes this composition rather than an attachment.
 *
 *  A PANEL IS A DOOR, and that is the whole protocol of this band. Pressing one goes to that
 *  evening's page; nothing here opens an overlay. The landing's job is to send a reader onward,
 *  and a surface that answers the strongest press with a bigger picture is the dead end the
 *  image archive was built to fix. The frame (islands/landing/ImageLightbox) belongs where a
 *  reader has already declared they want to look at photographs — /obrazy and the concert
 *  galleries — so this page mounts neither the island nor its delegate.
 *
 *  IT IS A LINE, NEVER A GRID. A sixth evening gains a sixth frame, not a second row — a wall of
 *  thumbnails is the reflex answer and the one that damages the site (docs/web-imagines-spec §2:
 *  these are low-contrast chiaroscuro frames from a dark nave). The panels are uniform SQUARES,
 *  and that is arithmetic rather than taste twice over: a 3:2 nave keeps two thirds of its width
 *  at 1:1 where 4:5 kept barely half, and five cells across a fixed measure stand `measure / Σ
 *  aspect` tall — so hanging the five by their own ratios (1.50 · 1.50 · 1.50 · 0.80 · 1.50) is
 *  a line 232px tall at the measure and 190px on a laptop, with the portrait 152px wide. Variety
 *  of shape is paid for in the one dimension §2 is about. The crop is taken at BUILD
 *  (lib/croppedShot) rather than by `object-fit` in the browser — read that file for why. If a
 *  crop lands badly the levers are the evening's `framePosition` and its `frame`, both in
 *  data/landing/paths.
 *
 *  THE PLATE IS A PRINTED PLATE ON A PAGE, not a room of its own. It stands on the same parchment
 *  as the film above it and the register below, ringed by one hairline and lifted by the film's
 *  own shadow (`.vplayer-stage`), with the page itself showing through the 2px between cells — a
 *  codex divides a plate by scoring it, and on paper the score is the paper. The band used to cut
 *  the ground to night for its own length, which is a room change performed mid-movement by an
 *  object rather than by a threshold: on this landing the ground turns at the interludes and
 *  nowhere else.
 *
 *  THE HEAD KEEPS THE REGISTER'S SPINE; ONLY THE PLATE IS FULL-MEASURE. The band and the register
 *  are one movement, so they share one skeleton: `.section-grid`, rubric in the left rail under a
 *  hairline, voice in the body column. Earlier passes read `.path` as hanging everything from a
 *  single left edge and copied that — it does not. `.path` puts a 10px rubric at the measure's
 *  left and its title, its lede and every hairline in the register 500px further in, so a band
 *  that set its own voice at the rubric's edge was the only heading on the landing standing in a
 *  column of labels, and the reader met three consecutive objects on three left edges: the film
 *  centred at 880, the plate at the measure, the register's rules 495px right of it. The plate
 *  itself stays at the measure, because a full-measure illustration under a head set in the text
 *  column is a plate on a page — which is the one thing this object has always claimed to be.
 *
 *  ONE READOUT, NOT FIVE LABELS. Each frame carries its evening's name, positioned into the
 *  plate's foot and printed only while that frame is approached (`:hover` / `:focus-within`,
 *  no script). Five permanent captions under five cells is a card grid, and it prints the
 *  register's own index 200px above the register. On touch there is nothing to approach, so the
 *  same element sits statically under its panel in the rail — where one panel fills the screen,
 *  so it labels a photograph rather than indexing a list.
 *
 *  THE FOOT IS A ROW AT REST, NOT HALF OF ONE. The readout only exists while a panel is
 *  approached, so a foot holding nothing else printed one 10px reference alone against 1400px of
 *  empty paper — which is a "see all" under a shelf of cards, the exact reading the printed
 *  reference was introduced to kill. The left end now carries the plate's own caption, in the
 *  film's voice and slot, and the per-panel readout cross-fades with it: the plate always has a
 *  caption, and approaching a frame swaps the whole plate's for that evening's.
 *
 *  Registers: the frames are LIT (`.reveal-light`, the veil belongs to the image) and the head is
 *  INKED — never both on one node. The numeral rides UNDER the veil, because it is printed on the
 *  panel and arrives with it. The foot takes none: a half-ink control is a control the reader is
 *  not sure they may press.
 *
 *  THE PHOTOGRAPH IS THE SUBJECT HERE, not a ground, and that sentence decides the whole grade. It
 *  carries no scrim — every other full-bleed photograph on this landing wears one because words are
 *  set over it, and the only thing set over these is one numeral, which carries its own halo. It
 *  keeps no `contrast` at all, shared or per panel, and 14-imagines.css records both attempts and
 *  why each failed. Exposure and chroma are corrected PER EVENING (`frameLift` /
 *  `frameSaturation`, data/landing/paths), because no single number equalises five buildings shot
 *  by four photographers. What a filter cannot reach at all is a grade the photographer baked in
 *  unequally across the channels: that one is corrected in the asset (`frameAsset`).
 *
 *  THE FRAMES ARE CHOSEN ON SUBJECT SCALE, and that is the statistic three earlier passes never
 *  took. They picked frame by frame on luminance, which is why the line ran close · close · wide ·
 *  very wide · mid — its two emptiest panels back to back in the middle, where the eye lands
 *  first, and the ensemble unrecognisable in both. The rule now is that every panel carries the
 *  architecture AND the singers at a size a reader can read: five frames that look made by one eye
 *  at one remove. Consistency, never a pattern — an arrangement in five items (two close wings
 *  around three wide, say) reads as a device, and devices are what makes a plate look generated.
 *  Verify a candidate by rendering the line at 316px under the real grade, never by reading `alt`.
 * @architecture Astro islands 2026
 * @module components/landing/ImaginesBand
 */
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import { PATHS, ROMAN } from "../../data/landing/paths";
import { croppedShot, type CroppedShot } from "../../lib/croppedShot";
import { photoOptional } from "../../lib/photos";

/** The panel's shape, and it must agree with `aspect-ratio` in 14-imagines.css — the renditions
 *  are cut to this, so a sheet that changed alone would letterbox every frame in the line. */
const PANEL_ASPECT = 1;

/** Panel widths in CSS pixels, covering every regime `sizes` below declares: 360 the whole 1×
 *  line (230px at the 1280 breakpoint, 316px once the plate reaches the measure), 480 both the
 *  1× tablet cap and a 2× line at 1280, 640 a 2× line at the measure and a 2× phone rail, 960
 *  both a 3× phone and the 420px tablet cap at 2×. Four rungs, and each has to land close: an
 *  honest `sizes` means the browser takes the smallest candidate at or above the need, so a gap
 *  in the ladder is paid in full. The one case the ladder deliberately does not reach is a 3×
 *  tablet holding the cap (1260px needed) — that device gets the 960 stretched 31%, which is
 *  cheaper than a fifth rung emitted for all five evenings to serve it. */
const PANEL_WIDTHS = [360, 480, 640, 960];

interface BandFrame {
  readonly roman: string;
  /** The night this photograph was taken, printed in the readout ("wrzesień MMXXIV"). */
  readonly date: string;
  /** This panel's corrections to the shared grade as inline custom properties, or "" for a panel
   *  the line's own grade already fits. Assembled here rather than in the markup because both
   *  levers are optional and independent, and an empty `style` attribute is not. */
  readonly grade: string;
  readonly title: string;
  readonly concertId: string;
  readonly alt: string;
  /** The panel itself: already square, so `sizes` may state the panel's own width (croppedShot). */
  readonly panel: CroppedShot;
}

// The register is the band's index, so the band walks PATHS rather than the concerts collection:
// an evening the register does not print (the Bobola liturgy) has no frame here either, and the
// numerals cannot drift from the ones printed below. The photographs themselves still come from
// the collection — that is where they live.
const byId = new Map((await getCollection("concerts")).map((c) => [c.id, c.data] as const));

const frames: BandFrame[] = [];
for (const [i, path] of PATHS.entries()) {
  const gallery = byId.get(path.concertId)?.gallery ?? [];
  const entry = path.frame ? gallery.find((g) => g.img === path.frame) : gallery[0];
  if (path.frame && !entry) {
    throw new Error(
      `[imagines] "${path.frame}" is not in the "${path.concertId}" gallery. The band's frame must` +
        ` be one of that evening's own photographs, so its alt comes with it.`,
    );
  }
  // `frame` names the gallery entry — that is where alt, credit and run come from, and the check
  // above holds it to the evening's own photographs. `frameAsset` swaps only the FILE that gets
  // cropped, for the one evening whose photograph needs a correction the browser cannot apply
  // (data/landing/paths). The two are deliberately separate: a band that took its provenance from
  // whatever file it happened to render could caption a corrected copy as if nothing had changed.
  const img = entry ? photoOptional(path.frameAsset ?? entry.img) : undefined;
  // An evening with no photographs simply has no frame — the numbering then has an honest gap
  // rather than a placeholder.
  if (!entry || !img) continue;
  frames.push({
    roman: ROMAN[i],
    date: path.frameDate,
    grade: [
      path.frameSaturation === undefined ? null : `--panel-sat:${path.frameSaturation}`,
      path.frameLift === undefined ? null : `--panel-lift:${path.frameLift}`,
    ]
      .filter(Boolean)
      .join(";"),
    title: path.title,
    concertId: path.concertId,
    alt: entry.alt ?? "",
    panel: await croppedShot(img, {
      aspect: PANEL_ASPECT,
      widths: PANEL_WIDTHS,
      position: path.framePosition,
    }),
  });
}

// The plate's caption, printed at the left of the foot whenever no single panel is being read.
// DERIVED, so it cannot drift from the line it describes: the span is the first and last frame's
// own year, which is a fact about the PHOTOGRAPHS — the register dates the programmes, and three
// of these five evenings share a year there. A set collapses the range when one year holds all of
// them. It states the genre rather than the count: "five" over five visible panels is the reader's
// own arithmetic read back to them, and it would need re-declining the moment a sixth arrives.
const years = frames.map((f) => f.date.split(" ").at(-1) ?? "");
const plateSpan = [...new Set([years[0], years.at(-1)])].filter(Boolean).join("–");
---

{
  frames.length > 0 && (
    <section class="imagines" aria-label="Obrazy wieczorów" data-spine-clear>
      {/* One box carries the page's measure for everything in the band, the way `.path` does one
          section below. The plate and the foot take that measure whole; the head divides it on
          `.section-grid`, which is the register's own skeleton and the reason the two sections
          read as one movement. */}
      <div class="imagines-inner">
        <div class="section-grid imagines-head">
          {/* The rubric's shape is VoxMoment's, directly above: the Latin tier and its gloss on
              one line, separated by a mid-dot in the text. base.css owns both voices — this band
              names no face of its own. It takes `.section-label` for its SLOT, which is where the
              register prints "Z drogi" 400px below, under the same hairline; two rubrics in one
              rail is a register's rhythm, and it carries no `.reveal` for the same reason that
              one does not — the rail is the page's furniture, the voice beside it is the arrival. */}
          <div class="section-label micro">
            <span><span class="lat">Imagines</span> · Obrazy wieczorów</span>
          </div>
          {/* The band's voice. Every other surface on this landing sets one line — even the
              wordless photograph section ("Światło prowadzi słuchacza.") — and a surface that says
              nothing where everything else speaks reads as a component standing on the rite.

              THE STEP IS ALWAYS FORWARD. Movement II runs silence → voice → memory — that is what
              `Vox memoriae` names — and the band stands exactly where the movement turns to the
              third term. Naming the silence again here (the line this slot used to hold) sends the
              reader back two sections to a term the page has already spent, and claims of
              photographs of a sung evening the one thing they visibly are not. The subject is what
              the voice leaves behind, never the sound itself: the register below owns that ("Co już
              zabrzmiało."). Set a grade under `.section-title`, or movement II is three titled
              sections in a row.

              It echoes the film's "Z tej ciszy — głos." in syntax, and that is a family resemblance
              rather than a couplet: earlier passes called the two a distich and set them on
              different axes at different sizes with a 495px film between them, which is not a
              distich to any reader. The line's job is to carry this section, not to complete the
              one before it. */}
          <p class="imagines-line reveal">
            Z tego głosu — <em>pamięć.</em>
          </p>
        </div>

        {/* `no-snap`: the magnetic pull toward a target's centre used to be off here by
            construction, because the cursor's frame state sits above the branch that applies it.
            These panels are ordinary links now, and a 316px square would pull the pointer 15%
            toward its own middle — over a picture that reads as the cursor being taken away from
            what it is pointing at (islands/landing/hooks/useSiteCursor). */}
        <div class="imagines-plate" data-cursor="no-snap">
          {frames.map((f) => (
            <figure class="imagines-frame">
              <a
                class="imagines-media plausible-event-name=imagines+wieczor"
                href={`/koncerty/${f.concertId}`}
              >
                {/* A plain `<img>`, not `<Image>`: the renditions are cut to the panel's square up
                    in the frontmatter, and the component has no way to ask for a crop and a ladder
                    at once — `widths` derives its own aspect from the source and would drop the one
                    thing these files exist for.

                    `sizes` states the panel exactly, in all FOUR of its regimes: the rail's 74vw
                    while that is under the cap, the 420px cap above it, the line's share of a plate
                    still narrower than the measure, and its fixed share once the plate reaches the
                    measure and stops growing. It may be read literally, which is the whole point of
                    the build-time crop — a browser-side `cover` would have drawn each of these wider
                    than the panel and made every honest number here an understatement.

                    Both plate numbers are arithmetic, not guesses. Below the measure the plate is
                    `100vw - 2 × 5vw`, so five panels divide 90vw and each is 18vw; at and above it
                    the plate is pinned to 1580px and each panel is a flat 316px. (Both ignore the
                    12px the scoring takes out of the row, so the panel measures 313.6 and this
                    overstates it by 0.8% — which changes no candidate on either rung of the ladder,
                    and errs toward the sharper file.) The two meet at 1756px of viewport, which is
                    where 90vw first exceeds
                    1580 — so the boundary is continuous rather than a step. 76 rather than 74 is the
                    one deliberate rounding: below a 321px viewport the panel stops shrinking at its
                    238px floor, which is 74.4vw. Every breakpoint here is the sheet's own
                    (14-imagines.css); they move together or not at all. */}
                <img
                  src={f.panel.src}
                  srcset={f.panel.srcset}
                  sizes="(max-width: 567px) 76vw, (max-width: 1279px) 420px, (max-width: 1755px) 18vw, 316px"
                  width={f.panel.width}
                  height={f.panel.height}
                  alt={f.alt}
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                  style={f.grade || undefined}
                />
                {/* The plate number, printed on the frame the way an engraving carries one. It is
                    what ties this line to the register's numbering without a caption row, and it
                    rides under the veil because it belongs to the panel and arrives with it. */}
                <span class="imagines-roman" aria-hidden="true">
                  {f.roman}
                </span>
                <span class="reveal-light" aria-hidden="true" />
                {/* The door has to say where it leads for a reader who cannot see the photograph;
                    the readout below is a pointer affordance and reaches nobody here. */}
                <span class="sr-only">
                  {f.title} · {f.date}
                </span>
              </a>
              {/* The readout. Hidden from assistive tech because the link above already carries
                  the same name — this element exists to print it for the eye.

                  The date OWNS the mid-dot in front of it rather than standing beside a loose one:
                  on the rail the date drops to a line of its own (14-imagines.css) and a separator
                  that belonged to the row would be left hanging at the end of the name. */}
              <figcaption class="imagines-name" aria-hidden="true">
                <span class="imagines-name-n">{f.roman}</span>
                <span class="imagines-name-dot">·</span>
                {f.title}
                <span class="imagines-name-date">
                  <span class="imagines-name-dot">·</span>
                  {f.date}
                </span>
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
          ))}
        </div>

        {/* The plate's foot is the film's foot: the same mono voice, a caption at the left and the
            reference at the right, exactly where the player above prints its caption and its clock.
            The reference is PRINTED, not called for — the arrow and the rule of `.primary-link` are
            what made a row of photographs read as a card shelf with a "see all" under it.

            The caption and the per-panel readout share this slot and cross-fade (14-imagines.css);
            the readout is positioned into it from inside the plate, so the two are siblings on one
            baseline rather than one box that swaps its text. */}
        <div class="imagines-foot">
          <p class="imagines-caption">Fotografie wieczorów cyklu · {plateSpan}</p>
          <a
            class="imagines-all plausible-event-name=imagines+wszystkie"
            href="/obrazy"
          >
            Wszystkie obrazy
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  )
}
